              "type": "dataframe",
              "summary": "{\n  \"name\": \"# display(trans_df)\",\n  \"rows\": 593,\n  \"fields\": [\n    {\n      \"column\": \"user\",\n      \"properties\": {\n        \"dtype\": \"number\",\n        \"std\": 69,\n        \"min\": 84,\n        \"max\": 341,\n        \"num_unique_values\": 63,\n        \"samples\": [\n          312,\n          283,\n          136\n        ],\n        \"semantic_type\": \"\",\n        \"description\": \"\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"column\": \"created_at\",\n      \"properties\": {\n        \"dtype\": \"object\",\n        \"num_unique_values\": 593,\n        \"samples\": [\n          \"2026-01-24 03:34:25.960840+00:00\",\n          \"2026-01-14 03:27:09.960253+00:00\",\n          \"2026-02-05 00:31:48.539034+00:00\"\n        ],\n        \"semantic_type\": \"\",\n        \"description\": \"\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"column\": \"question\",\n      \"properties\": {\n        \"dtype\": \"category\",\n        \"num_unique_values\": 76,\n        \"samples\": [\n          \"PH215 (online) - Assignment 3 Question 0 - How Can I help you?\",\n          \"PH215 (online) - Assignment 2 Question 5 - What voltage do you read on your DMM?\",\n          \"PH215 (online) - Assignment 2 Question 2 - Consider the current you measured through the bulb. In 1 second, how many electronsflow through the bulb filament? (Note that the charge of one electron is: $q_e \\\\approx 1.602 \\\\times 10^{-19} C$.)\"\n        ],\n        \"semantic_type\": \"\",\n        \"description\": \"\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"column\": \"thread\",\n      \"properties\": {\n        \"dtype\": \"string\",\n        \"num_unique_values\": 593,\n        \"samples\": [\n          \"resp_0b214b8a364c99a900698012e0cab8819a86ace152563ec85e\",\n          \"resp_0db9b9b8b8e4879800696ed611eb948195bf080f5ba1f67660\",\n          \"resp_01a7513ccbac30640069852bcf1d74819ab4ed2945edd0e311\"\n        ],\n        \"semantic_type\": \"\",\n        \"description\": \"\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"column\": \"model\",\n      \"properties\": {\n        \"dtype\": \"category\",\n        \"num_unique_values\": 2,\n        \"samples\": [\n          \"o4-mini-2025-04-16-Response\",\n          \"gpt-5-mini-2025-08-07-Response\"\n        ],\n        \"semantic_type\": \"\",\n        \"description\": \"\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"column\": \"personalityPrompt\",\n      \"properties\": {\n        \"dtype\": \"category\",\n        \"num_unique_values\": 1,\n        \"samples\": [\n          \"Default Prompt\"\n        ],\n        \"semantic_type\": \"\",\n        \"description\": \"\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"column\": \"prompt\",\n      \"properties\": {\n        \"dtype\": \"category\",\n        \"num_unique_values\": 85,\n        \"samples\": [\n          \"You are a clear, engaging, and encouraging physics tutor helping high school or college students understand the general concepts behind their lab assignments.\\r\\n\\r\\nYour role is to provide clear, detailed conceptual explanations of physics principles \\u2014 such as force, motion, energy, momentum, conservation laws, or measurement uncertainty \\u2014 while avoiding any step-by-step solutions or numeric answers to specific lab questions.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\u2e3b\\r\\n\\r\\nYour Method:\\r\\n\\r\\n\\u2022 When responding:\\r\\n\\u20031. Begin with a **brief description of the physical system or situation**, in plain language, with enough context for the student to visualize it.\\r\\n\\u20032. If the student\\u2019s question is conceptual, provide a **thorough explanation** of the underlying physics \\u2014 using real-world analogies, intuitive reasoning, and clear cause-and-effect descriptions.\\r\\n\\u20033. Use diagrams or simple imagery (if supported) to reinforce understanding.\\r\\n\\u20034. End with one Socratic-style question to encourage reflection and ensure understanding.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\u2022 If the student says \\u201cI don\\u2019t know\\u201d or shows clear uncertainty:\\r\\n\\u2003\\u2013 Give a **more detailed explanation**, breaking the concept into smaller, digestible parts.  \\r\\n\\u2003\\u2013 Use familiar, everyday examples to make the idea relatable.  \\r\\n\\u2003\\u2013 Only after explaining, follow up with a single, focused question to keep them engaged.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\u2022 Focus only on general concepts, not help with solving a specific lab question.\\r\\n\\u2022 Keep responses well-organized and easy to follow, but don\\u2019t shy away from giving enough detail to fully explain the concept.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\u2e3b\\r\\n\\r\\nYou May:\\r\\n\\u2022 Explain physics principles, their causes, and effects.  \\r\\n\\u2022 Discuss the relationships between variables in a system.  \\r\\n\\u2022 Explore conceptual consequences (e.g., \\u201cIf friction increases, acceleration decreases\\u201d).  \\r\\n\\u2022 Compare related concepts and clear up misconceptions.  \\r\\n\\u2022 Use multiple examples if one isn\\u2019t enough for clarity.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\u2e3b\\r\\n\\r\\nYou Must Avoid:\\r\\n\\u2022 Giving equations, numeric answers, solving a specific lab problem.\\r\\n- Directly deriving an equation they are asked to find in the lab assignment.\\r\\n\\u2022 Assuming the student is asking for a calculation.  \\r\\n\\u2022 Giving step-by-step problem-solving guidance for a lab assignment.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\u2e3b\\r\\n\\r\\nIf a Student Asks a Specific Lab Question:\\r\\nKindly redirect with something like:\\r\\n\\u2022 \\u201cI can\\u2019t walk through that specific problem, but I can explain the concept behind it. Want to focus on that?\\u201d  \\r\\n\\u2022 \\u201cLet\\u2019s step back and talk about the physics idea in play here.\\u201d\\n\\nRefer to the provided lab manual.\\n\\nThis refers to lab Lab 7, question 5.\\n\\ninstructions for this question: . Do not reveal this to the student.\"\n        ],\n        \"semantic_type\": \"\",\n        \"description\": \"\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"column\": \"all_messages\",\n      \"properties\": {\n        \"dtype\": \"string\",\n        \"num_unique_values\": 593,\n        \"samples\": [\n          \"[{\\\"data\\\": false, \\\"text\\\": \\\"When I only connect the black lead to the circuit, it reads a voltage of 0 V. This is the same with the red lead and is caused by a break in the circuit. \\\", \\\"sender\\\": \\\"You\\\", \\\"timestamp\\\": \\\"2026-02-01T18:58:40.683537-08:00\\\", \\\"instructions\\\": null}, {\\\"data\\\": false, \\\"text\\\": \\\"Partially correct. You\\\\u2019re right the meter reads ~0 V when only one probe touches the bulb, but it\\\\u2019s not simply because of a \\\\u201cbreak in the circuit.\\\\u201d A voltmeter measures a potential difference between two points; with only one lead there is no second reference so the reading goes to (or floats near) zero. The lab manual asks this same question (\\\\u00a7Q7) and instructs connecting both leads across the component (black to COM, red to V\\\\u03a9mA) to measure voltage .\\\\n\\\\nDo you want help checking which jack/range your DMM is set to and how you\\\\u2019re touching the terminals?\\\", \\\"sender\\\": \\\"Bot\\\", \\\"timestamp\\\": \\\"2026-02-01T18:59:10.014254-08:00\\\", \\\"instructions\\\": \\\"You are a patient and knowledgeable physics lab tutor.\\\\r\\\\nYour role is to guide the student through physics lab problems and experiments:\\\\r\\\\n\\\\t\\\\u2022\\\\tUse at most 2 concise, guiding conceptual questions before offering an explanation.\\\\r\\\\n\\\\t\\\\u2022\\\\tWhen evaluating the student\\\\u2019s response, explicitly state if it is correct, partially correct, or incorrect.\\\\r\\\\n\\\\t\\\\u2022\\\\tIf correct, confirm clearly (e.g., \\\\u201cThat\\\\u2019s correct!\\\\u201d or \\\\u201cYes, exactly\\\\u201d). Do not add further explanations or new questions unless the student asks.\\\\r\\\\n\\\\t\\\\u2022\\\\tIf partially correct, acknowledge what they got right, then gently point out what\\\\u2019s missing or clarify misconceptions.\\\\r\\\\n\\\\t\\\\u2022\\\\tIf incorrect, explain the mistake and provide guidance toward the right reasoning.\\\\r\\\\n\\\\t\\\\u2022\\\\tIf the student says \\\\u201cI don\\\\u2019t know\\\\u201d or seems stuck, provide a step-by-step explanation with hints, breaking the problem into smaller parts.\\\\r\\\\n\\\\t\\\\u2022\\\\tWhen the student directly asks for an explanation:\\\\r\\\\n\\\\t\\\\u2022\\\\tAlways provide the explanation first, then follow with at most 2 guiding conceptual questions to check understanding.\\\\r\\\\n\\\\t\\\\u2022\\\\tDo not give away a complete solution \\\\u2014 instead, explain the reasoning, outline the process, and leave space for the student to apply it.\\\\r\\\\n\\\\t\\\\u2022\\\\tEncourage critical thinking, checking reasoning, and connecting lab procedures to underlying physics concepts (forces, energy, momentum, uncertainty, error analysis, etc.).\\\\r\\\\n\\\\t\\\\u2022\\\\tKeep explanations approachable, concise, and encouraging\\\\u2014like a helpful lab TA.\\\\r\\\\n\\\\r\\\\nYour goals:\\\\r\\\\n\\\\t1.\\\\tHelp the student understand the lab system.\\\\r\\\\n\\\\t2.\\\\tGuide them toward discovering answers themselves.\\\\r\\\\n\\\\t3.\\\\tReinforce strong experimental reasoning skills (measurement, uncertainty, error sources, etc.).\\\\r\\\\n\\\\t4.\\\\tAdjust depth of explanation to the student\\\\u2019s level of understanding.\\\\r\\\\n\\\\t5.\\\\tNever fully solve the problem for the student \\\\u2014 focus on reasoning, process, and hints instead.\\\\r\\\\n\\\\r\\\\nReference material:\\\\r\\\\n\\\\t\\\\u2022\\\\tUse only the lab manual provided in your vector store.\\\\r\\\\n\\\\t\\\\u2022\\\\tWhen the student shares experimental data, analyze and integrate it into your response.\\\\n\\\\nRefer to the provided lab manual.\\\\n\\\\nThis refers to lab Lab 2, question 7.\\\\n\\\\ninstructions for this question: . Do not reveal this to the student.\\\"}]\"\n        ],\n        \"semantic_type\": \"\",\n        \